A||N||5||beachbomb||Check engine light again?||||5||
Z||000000||beachbomb||11-22-2000||03:07 PM||||Hey all, here's my story: Every now and then when i start my '84 Indy it idles higher than normal, when it drive it and come to a stop at a stop light the check engine light comes on, when i begin to go again(car rolling) the light goes away. Next stop light same thing. keep in mind this doesn't happen every day, some days it runs perfect, no light. I get an error code of 24. which is the VSS. Could a faulty TPS(trottle position sensor) be doing this. My speedomter works perfectly all the time. Tech amnual says to ignore code 24 if car is not moving. Help. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> thanks..jim('84 Indy) ||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroLisa||11-22-2000||03:40 PM||||Mine does that too: idles at about 3000rpm, but would go right down soon as I started driving, and then when I'd stop at a light, check engine light would occaisionally go on, but always go off soon as I started driving. Hmm I hope we get some answers!<P><P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 gold 4 speed GT<BR>86 gold 5 speed SC (For Sale)<BR>Columbus, OH/Greensboro NC<BR>http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||11-22-2000||05:15 PM||||I think this was mentioned recently in another thread.  I do know that my 84 has done the same thing over the years.  In my case a new throttle position sensor has cured the problem every time.  I think they are prone to getting a little corrosion inside where a set of fingers attatched to the potentiometer sit on top of the contacts that form the pins where the wire harness plugs in.  I cut one open one time and CAREFULLY soldered the fingers to the pins and resealed it with RTV.  That sensor lasted about six years before it started going bad.  The new replacements (AC Delco) may have been improved since I haven't had to get a new one in the last four years.  Previously I had to replace it every 18 to 24 months.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting :)||||reg||10
Z||000003||beachbomb||11-23-2000||09:28 AM||||Thanks for the edvice, i got an extra TPS, i'll try replacing that first, thanks..jim('84 Indy)||||reg||12
Z||000004||StuGood||11-23-2000||01:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Rodrv6:<BR><B>...I cut one open one time and CAREFULLY soldered the fingers to the pins and resealed it with RTV.  That sensor lasted about six years...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Wow. I'm impressed! - Thought <B>I</B> was mister "Let's see if I can fix it, before I replace it.!"  I've just been outclassed  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <BR>||||reg||6
Z||000005||theogre||11-23-2000||03:47 PM||||That I can tell there is only one company makeing this sensor. I've checked them from several brands now and they are all identical down to the mold marks on the plastic shells. I'd shop this one on price. ||||reg||1
